  "final_response": "- Using `--hard` makes `git reset` dangerous because it overwrites the working directory to match the index, discarding any uncommitted changes and undoing the last commit, staged changes, and all local work[reset.010-step-3-updating-the-working-directory].\n\n- To discard all local changes, run `git reset --hard` in your terminal. This resets both the index and the working directory to match the repository’s HEAD, forcibly overwriting any modifications.\n\n[SAFETY WARNING] (HIGH RISK): The command(s) above (`git reset --hard`) are destructive and cannot be easily undone.\n**Safer alternative:** **Risky command detected:** `git reset --hard` (see [reset.010-step-3-updating-the-working-directory-hard]).\n\n**Safer workflow:**  \n1. If you only want to undo the last commit but keep the changes staged, run `git reset --soft HEAD~1`.  \n2. If you need to discard the changes entirely, first create a backup with `git stash push -m \"backup before hard reset\"` and then use `git reset --hard HEAD~1`.  \n3. To revert a single file to its last committed state, use `git checkout -- <file>` instead of a hard reset.",
